**Theorem 2.2 (A TP-preserving fixed-encoder descent with exact error).**
For every $0\le\delta\le1$, the encoder

$$
B_f=\sqrt{c_f}\,b_f^TD^\dagger,\qquad
\operatorname{Enc}(X)=(B_fXB_f^\dagger)_f,
\qquad \operatorname{Dec}_{f,\delta}(y)=yD\sigma_{f,\delta}D^\dagger
\tag{2.6}
$$

is fixed independently of $\delta$, input and future policy, and
realizes $T_\delta\in\mathfrak C_1$. Every decoder is CPTP on all
of $M_1$. The normalized output-first Choi marginal is always $I_5/5$.

For

$$
0<\delta<\frac{41}{12500000},
\tag{2.7}
$$

the exact original error is $e(T_\delta)=\lambda_\delta<\lambda_Q$.
To specify these numbers, let

$$
\begin{gathered}
D_0=64468731767080,\\
a=\frac{32710322397343+193561738103r}{D_0},\quad
b=\frac{1674636624370+414723353026r}{D_0},\quad
c=\frac{-6400945486418-13799938410r}{D_0},\\
C_Q=\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\b&c\end{pmatrix},\qquad
P_+=R_+|_L,\qquad
C_\delta=C_Q-\delta\kappa P_+
=\begin{pmatrix}a_\delta&b_\delta\\b_\delta&c_\delta\end{pmatrix},\\
p_\delta(z)=z^2-(a_\delta+c_\delta)z
+a_\delta c_\delta-b_\delta^2,\\
\lambda_\delta=
\frac{a_\delta+c_\delta+
\sqrt{(a_\delta-c_\delta)^2+4b_\delta^2}}2,
\qquad \lambda_Q=\lambda_0.
\end{gathered}
\tag{2.8}
$$

The square root is nonnegative. In (2.7) the unique positive root
of $p_\delta$ is $\lambda_\delta$, and only the representative
event $\{N\}$ attains the fifteen-event maximum. For the fixed step
$\delta_*=1/400000$,

$$
\frac{524176444082}{10^{12}}
<\lambda_{\delta_*}
<\frac{524176444083}{10^{12}}
<\lambda_Q.
\tag{2.9}
$$

For every step (2.7), a pure input, actual wait zero, one complete
read and Stop attain $e(T_\delta)$.

Proof. The orthogonal sectors and
$1521^2+166^2\cdot119=5592605$,
$71^2\cdot119+94^2\cdot76=1271415$ make all vectors in (2.1) unit.
Circle second moments give the exact encoder normalization

$$
\begin{aligned}
\sum_{\mathrm A}c_fb_fb_f^T&=\frac{81}{119}R_++R_-,\\
\sum_{\mathrm B}c_fb_fb_f^T&=R_0+\frac{38}{119}R_+,
\qquad \sum_fc_fb_fb_f^T=I_5.
\end{aligned}
\tag{2.10}
$$

Every $\sigma_{f,\delta}$ is positive with trace one. For B flags,
decoder Kraus columns are $\sqrt{1-\delta}Da_{\mathrm B,k}$ and
$\sqrt\delta Dw_+(\beta_k)$; their squared-adjoint sum is one.
For A there is one norm-one column. Matrix sandwiches prove CP at
every reference dimension, and these normalizations prove TP on every
complex matrix in each whole domain. The encoder is unchanged. Its
normalized Choi matrix is

$$
\chi_\delta=\frac15\sum_fc_f\sigma_{f,\delta}\otimes b_fb_f^T,
\qquad \operatorname{Tr}_{\rm out}\chi_\delta=I_5/5.
\tag{2.11}
$$

There are fourteen scalar flags with mixed B decoding, and, for
$0<\delta<1$, nineteen nonzero rank-one composite Kraus terms.
Equivalently split the five B effects in proportions $1-\delta,\delta$
to obtain nineteen pure-decoder flags. No mixture record reaches the
future policy.

For stationarity, the credited A tensor has planar bidegrees
$(4,0),(3,1),(2,2),(1,3),(0,4)$. At even degrees the three-point
rule retains only zero frequency; in odd bidegrees its degree-one
factor has zero mean, eliminating the degree-three alias. Every old
or new B tensor has circle frequencies between $-4$ and $4$, so the
five-point rule is exactly its circle integral. These are identities
of coefficient tensors on all complex matrices. Angle translation
proves rotation covariance. Reflection reverses the A angles and,
up to irrelevant projector signs, sends B angle $\beta$ to
$\pi-\beta$. Thus the finite $S_\delta$ is stationary and reflected.
No continuous flag or new future angle is used.

The B preparation dark population $g$ is replaced by plus population
in proportion $\delta$. Its measurement populations and total orbit
weight remain $(119/195,76/195,0)$ and $195/119$. This gives (2.4).
Every column of its correction sums to zero, so the three column sums
of $\Lambda_\delta$ remain $(1,2,2)$. In QRC notation,
$M_{ij}=(\Lambda_\delta)_{ij}/\sqrt{d_id_j}$ and
$\tau=(1,\sqrt2,\sqrt2)^T$ obey $M^T\tau=\tau$. The six plane
coefficients, in QRC order $(+,-,2+,2-,\Delta,\Sigma)$, are

$$
\left((1-\delta)\frac{507224}{1271415},\ 0,\
\frac{(\Lambda_\delta)_{++}}4,\
\frac{(\Lambda_\delta)_{--}}4,\
\frac{2272374}{5592605},\frac{2272374}{5592605}\right).
\tag{2.12}
$$

The branch adjoint is
$E\mapsto c_fb_fb_f^T\operatorname{tr}(\sigma_{f,\delta}E)$.
Second and fourth circle moments give exactly (2.5), including its
transposed sector-transfer block. In particular $\mathcal A_\delta(I)=I$.
Actual positive product factors establish cone membership; no PPT
feasible point is used to infer it.

For every Hermitian effect $0\preceq E\preceq I$, each scalar
$\operatorname{tr}[(w_+w_+^T-a_{\mathrm B}a_{\mathrm B}^T)E]$
belongs to $[-1,1]$. Consequently, with
$M_{\mathrm B}=R_0+(38/119)R_+\preceq I$,

$$
-\delta M_{\mathrm B}\preceq
(S_\delta^\dagger-S_Q^\dagger)(E)
\preceq\delta M_{\mathrm B},\qquad
\|(S_\delta^\dagger-S_Q^\dagger)(E)\|_{\rm op}\le\delta.
\tag{2.13}
$$

This simultaneously protects every event of the same channel.
The credited exact QJM margins are

$$
\begin{gathered}
\|H_J(S_Q)\|_{\rm op}<h:=\frac{52417}{100000}
\quad(J\in\mathcal J_{15},\ J\ne\{N\}),\qquad
H_N(S_Q)\succ-I/2,\\
\ell:=\frac{52417656}{10^8}<\lambda_Q<\frac{52417658}{10^8}.
\end{gathered}
\tag{2.14}
$$

These are QJM Lemma 5.2 and Theorem 5.4, with all ten pairs retained.

For $E=\Pi_N$ the dark preparation component is invisible. Its branch
expectation increases by $gn\cos^2\beta$. The five-point moments
$\langle\cos^2\beta\rangle=1/2$,
$\langle\cos^4\beta\rangle=3/8$,
$\langle\cos^2\beta\sin^2\beta\rangle=1/8$ and the vanishing
odd moments yield

$$
\begin{aligned}
\Delta_N&:=\frac{(S_\delta^\dagger-S_Q^\dagger)(\Pi_N)}\delta\\
&=g\left[\frac n2R_0+
\frac{76}{119}\frac{nR_++2R_+\Pi_NR_+}{8}\right]\succeq0,
\qquad \Delta_N|_L=\kappa P_+.
\end{aligned}
\tag{2.15}
$$

The quotient denotes the fixed linear coefficient, including at
$\delta=0$. On $L$, $R_+\Pi_NR_+=nP_+$, proving (2.8).
The other coordinate block of $H_N(S_\delta)$ is a compression of
$-S_\delta^\dagger(\Pi_N)$, hence nonpositive.

The positive eigenvalue of $C_Q$ is simple. Its eigenvector is
proportional to $(b,\lambda_Q-a)$, with both entries positive since
$b>0$ and $\lambda_Q>a$. A generator of the plus line in $L$ also
has both entries positive by (1.6). This eigenvector is therefore not
in $\ker P_+$. Since $\kappa>0$, the Rayleigh equality condition in
$C_Q-\delta\kappa P_+\preceq C_Q$ gives
$\lambda_{\max}(C_\delta)<\lambda_Q$ for every $\delta>0$.
Indeed equality would require a maximizing vector in both the old
top eigenspace and $\ker P_+$.

The variational perturbation bound (2.13) gives
$\lambda_{\max}(C_\delta)\ge\lambda_Q-\delta>\ell-\delta$.
In (2.7),

$$
\ell-\delta>h+\delta,\qquad
\ell-\delta>\tfrac12+\delta,\qquad
\frac{\ell-h}{2}=\frac{41}{12500000}.
\tag{2.16}
$$

Every other event norm is below $h+\delta$ by (2.13)–(2.14), and
every negative $N$ eigenvalue has magnitude below $1/2+\delta$.
Thus the positive $L$ eigenvalue gives the whole fifteen-event maximum.
The negative $c_\delta$ and positive top eigenvalue imply
$\det C_\delta<0$, so $p_\delta$ has exactly one positive root.
Also $b>1/20$, $0<\kappa<1$ and $|(P_+)_{12}|<1$ give
$b_\delta>0$ throughout (2.7).

Here is an exact rational certificate for the stronger enclosure (2.9).
Integer squaring proves

$$
r_-:=\frac{4123105625617}{10^{12}}<r<
r_+:=\frac{4123105625618}{10^{12}}.
\tag{2.17}
$$

Reduce every field element by $r^2=17$ before applying

$$
\mathcal L(u+vr)=
\begin{cases}u+vr_-,&v\ge0,\\u+vr_+,&v<0,\end{cases}
\qquad
\mathcal U(u+vr)=
\begin{cases}u+vr_+,&v\ge0,\\u+vr_-,&v<0.\end{cases}
\tag{2.18}
$$

These are lower and upper bounds. Pair multiplication is
$(u,v)(u',v')=(uu'+17vv',uv'+vu')$. With
$l_d=524176444082/10^{12}$ and $u_d=524176444083/10^{12}$,
substitution in (2.8) at $\delta_*$ gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathcal U(p_{\delta_*}(l_d))
&=-\frac{3334113301631505048155681573392616917}
{7210963032484608868000000000000000000000000000000}<0,\\
\mathcal L(p_{\delta_*}(u_d))
&=\frac{1183978499237279836108665288159517233}
{7210963032484608868000000000000000000000000000000}>0.
\end{aligned}
\tag{2.19}
$$

The unique positive root lies between these rational endpoints, and
$u_d<\ell<\lambda_Q$. No convergence or sampled eigenvalue is used.

Finally (1.8), stationarity and TP make the fifteen-event maximum an
upper for every complex input and every actual finite history. Set

$$
v_\delta=
\frac{b_\delta e_N+(\lambda_\delta-a_\delta)e_{25}}
{\sqrt{b_\delta^2+(\lambda_\delta-a_\delta)^2}},
\qquad \rho_\delta=Dv_\delta v_\delta^TD^\dagger.
\tag{2.20}
$$

The denominator is nonzero. The characteristic equation gives
$C_\delta v_\delta=\lambda_\delta v_\delta$. At actual wait zero,
the full read has event-$N$ probability difference $\lambda_\delta$.
Its TV is at least this difference and at most the proved upper, so
equality holds. This is an actual read and Stop, with no closure-angle
or limiting-wait requirement. $\square$

